package builtin

// Package builtin is the compiler's builtins and runtime: the functions,
// types and values Tack provides without a declaration or an import, the
// C that some of them and lists run on, and the file that documents them
// all. It is a port of the Go compiler's package builtin, as far as the
// stages ported so far and the driver need it.
//
// # The builtins
//
// The resolver needs the builtins' names, to bind a call to one and to
// stop a program from declaring a function with a builtin's name; the
// loader, to stop a package from taking one; the type checker their
// signatures; and the C backend the C names of those with a C body. All
// of them read one Set, default_set, which is what keeps the stages from
// disagreeing about what the builtins are. Go's nil *Set, which has no
// builtins with a C body, as a dump of the tokens or the trees needs
// none, is no_set here: a Set is a flag saying whether it has them, since
// there is one set of builtins.
//
// print and exit have a C body, so a Func each: a name, its parameter's
// name and type, and the C name, tack_builtin_print and
// tack_builtin_exit, that a call lowers to. Go's Funcs is funcs_count and
// func_at, and Lookup is lookup. Each has one parameter, so a Func holds
// one; Go's holds a list.
//
// len, make, append, reserve, pop, remove, swap, replace, put, delete,
// has, drop, add, add_all, insert and fields are generic. Go's Generic is
// generic_count and generic_name, Names is names_count and name_at, and
// ParamNames is param_names. is_func_name says whether a name is any
// builtin function's of a Set, Names searched. Types and Values list the
// builtin types and values, true, false, noerror and c_allocator; here
// they are type_count and type_name, value_count and value_name. The
// builtin constraints, Data, are constraint_count and constraint_name,
// and is_constraint tells one by name.
//
// Called and Used, which find the builtins a resolved program calls, and
// UsesUnreachable, RunsC and UsesLists, which say what a program needs of
// the runtime, the C backend's and the driver's, are called, used,
// uses_unreachable, runs_c and uses_lists (see used.tck), each walking
// the program from a stack of its own.
//
// # The runtime and the documentation
//
// Go embeds the runtime's C files, their header and builtin.tck with
// go:embed. Tack has no such directive, so tools/embed writes their
// bytes into embedded.tck as string literals, from this repository's
// runtime directory, which holds the originals, and tackc's porttest
// checks that what it holds is those files byte for byte, that
// tools/embed writes it as it is, and that the runtime directory's
// files are still tackc's frozen copies:
//
//     tack --out bin/embed ./tools/embed
//     bin/embed builtin/embedded.tck builtin doc=runtime/builtin.tck \
//         header=runtime/tack/tack.h runtime[]=runtime/exit.c ...
//
// with every runtime C file in the byte order of their names, which
// make embed runs, and tackc's make port-embed through it.
// runtime_count and runtime_file are Go's
// RuntimeCFiles, in that order; runtime_header is RuntimeHeader, and
// header_path the path a C file includes it by. source is the
// builtins' documentation, Go's Source, which is never compiled;
// copy_differs and append_copy_error are Go's CopyError, which the
// driver's --check reports with. Go's Doc, which parses it for an
// editor, is not ported.
